{#
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  THE NOTICE — shown only when the operator has chosen to ask first
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Drawn only when {@see CookiePrefs::mustAsk()} is true, which means all four of:
  the operator set `visits_consent_mode` to `consent`, the tracker is switched on,
  the visitor has not already answered, and their browser has not answered for
  them. That last condition matters most — asking somebody to agree to something we
  have already decided not to do is a dark pattern, and it is one whether or not
  anybody intended it.

  ── WHY THE OPERATOR'S SETTING NEEDED A BANNER TO GO WITH IT ────────────────

  Because a `consent` mode with nowhere to consent is a switch that silently
  counts nobody for ever. The visitor is never asked, so the answer is never yes,
  so the report goes quiet and the screen says "nobody came". The mode and the
  question are one feature and had to ship together.

  ── NO DARK PATTERNS, AND THAT IS A LIST OF SPECIFICS ───────────────────────

  Both answers are one press, both are the same size, the same shape and the same
  weight, and neither is a link dressed as text. Nothing is pre-selected. The page
  behind stays readable and scrollable — this is not a modal and there is no
  overlay — because holding a policy page hostage until somebody agrees to be
  counted is the behaviour the rules exist to stop. It works with scripts off: it
  is a form, and it posts.

  Dismissing without answering is deliberately NOT offered. A close button that
  leaves the question unanswered means the notice returns on the next page, which
  trains people to click whichever button makes it go away — and that is the
  consent those designs actually collect.
#}
<style nonce="{{ csp_nonce }}">
  .cn{position:fixed;inset:auto 1rem 1rem 1rem;z-index:60;max-width:44rem;margin-inline:auto;
      background:#fff;border:1px solid #cfd6d4;border-radius:14px;
      box-shadow:0 12px 34px rgba(12,24,22,.16);padding:1.15rem 1.25rem}
  .cn__t{font:700 1.02rem/1.35 Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;color:#16211f;margin:0 0 .4rem}
  .cn__p{font-size:.92rem;line-height:1.55;color:#44514f;margin:0 0 1rem;max-width:62ch}
  .cn__p a{color:#16211f}
  .cn__row{display:flex;gap:.6rem;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center}
  /* Equal weight, and that is the requirement rather than the taste: the two
     buttons differ only in the word on them. */
  .cn__b{font:600 .92rem/1 inherit;padding:.68rem 1.1rem;border-radius:10px;cursor:pointer;
         border:1px solid #237b22;background:#fff;color:#16211f;flex:0 0 auto}
  .cn__b:hover{background:#f0f2f2}
  .cn__b:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #f3b416;outline-offset:2px}
  .cn__more{font-size:.86rem;color:#6b7472;margin-left:auto}
  @media (max-width:560px){
    .cn{inset:auto .6rem .6rem .6rem;padding:1rem}
    .cn__row{flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch}
    .cn__b{width:100%}
    .cn__more{margin:.5rem 0 0;text-align:center}
  }
</style>

<aside class="cn" role="region" aria-labelledby="cn-t">
  <p class="cn__t" id="cn-t">May we count this visit?</p>
  <p class="cn__p">
    One row, so that whoever shared the link you followed can find out whether it worked.
    It is ours alone &mdash; no Google Analytics, no advertising pixel, nothing that reports
    your visit to another company &mdash; and we keep no IP address.
    <a href="/cookies#counting-arrivals">What exactly is recorded</a>.
  </p>
  <form method="post" action="/cookies/choice" class="cn__row">
    <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token }}">
    <input type="hidden" name="return" value="{{ cookie_return|default('/') }}">
    <button type="submit" name="choice" value="yes" class="cn__b">Yes, count it</button>
    <button type="submit" name="choice" value="no" class="cn__b">No, thank you</button>
    <span class="cn__more">Either way, the site works the same.</span>
  </form>
</aside>
